# Experiment 14, the first answer: the same ore line, built in about 1790.
#
# Two wheels in the race, and a belt from each of them to each of the crushers:
#
#   river -> water wheel -> belt -> crusher
#
# Three things about that sentence are mechanical rather than decorative.
#
# 1. There is one prime mover for the whole plant, and everything is arranged
#    around where it is. A wheel cannot be moved to the machine; the machine
#    goes to the wheel. That is why this plant is a *line* and the electric one
#    is a row of islands.
#
# 2. The belts are not lossy pipes, they are isolation. A crusher shakes at 7
#    and a timber wheel rates 4, so a rigid drive from the wheel to the crusher
#    -- the obvious thing, and what a gearbox would have done -- pulls the
#    wheel apart on the first tick. Take the word `belt` off the four drive
#    wires in this file and both wheels stop with SHAKING, and the crushers
#    starve behind them and make nothing. The crushers themselves are never the
#    thing that fails -- they are on cast iron, which rates exactly 7 and not a
#    unit more. It is everything they are bolted to.
#
#    The second crusher is at 14,10 rather than 15 for that sentence's sake. A
#    belt reaches fourteen tiles and a rigid drive reaches twelve, so at 15 the
#    experiment would not be "the same plant, shaking" -- it would be a document
#    the compiler refuses, which proves something much less interesting.
#
# 3. Nothing here gets hot. There is no cooling in this design because there is
#    nothing to cool: a water wheel generates no heat at all and the crushers
#    settle at 60 degrees against a range that runs to 70. The first era's
#    problem is that it runs out of torque, not that it cooks.
#
# The belts used to be five-tile components and are now a word at the end of a
# wire, and this is the design that shows why that is the right way round.
# There is nothing on the ground called a belt. There is a *drive* between a
# wheel and a crusher, and it is slack, and being told so is the entire content
# of the decision. As components they also made the plot lie: the first-era
# plant was nearly twice the ground the electric one needed, and about half of
# that was belts and shaft standing in rows being counted as machinery.
#
# The pulley is the one first-era component this design does not use, and the
# reason is worth writing down: a pulley is a *ratio*, and a river turning at
# speed 1 already suits a crusher, which takes 2 or less. Ask the same era for
# the `crush` brief -- which needs a mill, and a mill wants speed 4 -- and the
# pulley train stops being optional.

machine "Water Ore Line"
brief line

pump      P1  at 0,0    draws water
pump      P2  at 0,4    draws water
waterwheel W1 at 4,0
waterwheel W2 at 4,5

crusher   C1  at 10,10  frame iron
crusher   C2  at 14,10  frame iron
outlet    O1  at 19,10

inlet     I1  at 6,10   draws ore

wire P1.water -> W1.water
wire P2.water -> W2.water

wire W1.rotary -> C1.drive  belt
wire W2.rotary -> C1.drive  belt
wire W1.rotary -> C2.drive  belt
wire W2.rotary -> C2.drive  belt

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> O1.solid
